  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Winning a chess game is a binomial experiment, but the number of times the player wins cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II follows a geometric distribution. Firing an arrow is a binomial experiment, and the number of arrows fired before the first arrow hits the bullseye can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Passing a calculus exam is a binomial experiment, but the number of students passing the exam cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
