  "proof_feedback": null,
  "html": "<div id=\"questionExplanation-89479\" class=\"questionExplanation\" style=\"display: block;\"><p>A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.</p>\n<p>With that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Random variable I does <em>not</em> follow a geometric distribution. Winning a chess game is a binomial experiment, but the number of times the player wins cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Random variable II follows a geometric distribution. Firing an arrow is a binomial experiment, and the number of arrows fired before the first arrow hits the bullseye can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Random variable III does <em>not</em> follow a geometric distribution. Passing a calculus exam is a binomial experiment, but the number of students passing the exam cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"</p>\n</div>",
